---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/content/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { siteConfig } from '../config/site';
import { profilePageJsonLd } from '../lib/seo';

const path = '/about/';
const crumbs = [{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'About', path }];
const author = siteConfig.author;
const jsonLd = [
  profilePageJsonLd({
    dateCreated: `${siteConfig.launchedAt}T00:00:00+05:00`,
    dateModified: '2026-10-20T00:00:00+05:00',
  }),
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About DocuForge Tools"
  description="DocuForge Tools is a free set of business document generators built and maintained by Hammad Iqbal. Honest about page, sources, and limits."
  path={path}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
  <h1>About {siteConfig.siteName}</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    {siteConfig.siteName} is a free set of business document generators—estimates, invoices,
    contracts, consent forms, and creative templates—built and maintained by one person: me,
    {author.name}.
  </p>

  <h2>Why I built it</h2>
  <p>
    I kept needing profession-specific paperwork (a construction estimate with allowances, a
    photography contract with usage rights, a cleaning invoice with rooms and frequency) and found
    either blank PDFs with no guidance, or template mills that hid the clauses behind a signup wall.
    I wanted fillable tools that run in the browser, show a live preview, export PDF or DOCX, and
    cite the public sources behind the educational copy—without uploading anyone’s client data.
  </p>

  <h2 id="maintainer">Who I am</h2>
  <p>
    {author.shortBio} This site isn’t a law firm, accounting practice, or licensed advisory service.
    What I do is turn published, authoritative sources (IRS, SBA, Cornell LII, HUD, CDC overviews,
    U.S. Copyright Office) into tools that are easy to use, and show exactly which sources each
    tool uses.
  </p>
  <p>
    Find me on
    <a href={author.canonicalPersonUrl} rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a>
    · Email <a href={`mailto:${author.email}`}>{author.email}</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is no photo here on purpose. Google’s guidance says not to invent headshots or credentials;
    I’d rather leave the image out than fake one.
  </p>

  <h2>How the tools are made</h2>
  <p>
    Every tool lists how totals are calculated (when applicable) and its sources at the bottom of
    the page. Sources are official pages (IRS, SBA, Cornell LII Wex/CFR, HUD, CDC, Copyright Office,
    FTC) that I have checked. Each page shows the date I last reviewed it against those sources.
    Full details: <a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What this site doesn’t do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>It doesn’t give legal, tax, or medical advice. Contract and consent pages are editable samples.</li>
    <li>Your form inputs stay in your browser. Optional drafts use localStorage on your device. Analytics cookies are described on the <a href="/cookies/">cookie notice</a>.</li>
    <li>It isn’t affiliated with the IRS, SBA, HUD, CDC, FTC, or any brand named in examples.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How the site is funded</h2>
  <p>
    The site is free. Google Analytics is configured for aggregate traffic. Google AdSense may be
    enabled later (currently off unless the publisher flag is set). There are no affiliate links today.
  </p>

  <h2>Other free tool sites I maintain</h2>
  <p>Same maintainer, different niches—listed here only, not in every footer:</p>
  <ul>
    {siteConfig.siblingSites.map((s) => (
      <li><a href={s.url} rel="noopener noreferrer">{s.name}</a></li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <h2>Found a mistake?</h2>
  <p>
    Email <a href={`mailto:${siteConfig.contactEmail}`}>{siteConfig.contactEmail}</a> with the page
    URL, what looks wrong, and a link to the source that shows the right information. Confirmed
    fixes are listed in the <a href="/methodology/#corrections">correction log</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
